# Codex Adversarial Review

Target: branch diff against origin/main
Verdict: needs-attention

Do not ship yet. Static review of 40b484b..e115c75: R-004 holds; all five requested paths trigger docs-check without enabling the full CI gate. R-005 holds; same-page checking and both-direction regression cases are present. R-006 partially holds, but pipeline directory tracking permits protected writes. R-007 fixes separate options and --, but misses valid option clusters. R-008 fixes the recorded read examples, but its sed classification still blocks reads and introduces a write bypass. The added cases exercise PASS and BLOCK directions, but omit these failures. No additional substantive defect found in e081d88's Step 10/11/12 documentation changes. New findings: four below. No files edited or builds/tests run; conclusions are static, not runtime verification.

Findings:
- [high] Pipeline-local cd changes leak into subsequent command tracking (.claude/hooks/guard-oracle.sh:101-104)
  R-006 is incomplete. From the repository root, `cd tests/golden; cd ../.. | cat; printf x > probe.hpp` passes both matchers by inspection. split_statements discards the pipeline boundary, and evaluate applies the piped cd to its persistent cur. Bash runs that cd in a pipeline subprocess, so the final printf actually remains in tests/golden while the guard checks the repository root. This violates the requirement to resolve writes against their effective directory.
  Recommendation: Preserve pipeline boundaries and prevent pipeline-local directory changes from updating the parent directory state. Add protected-write and harmless-write cases following pipelines.
- [high] Valid clustered cd options still bypass R-007 (.claude/hooks/guard-oracle.sh:248-259)
  `cd -Pe -- tests/golden && printf x > probe.hpp` is a valid Bash option form, but -Pe is absent from CD_OPTS. The helper treats it as an unrecognized option and leaves cur at the repository root; the literal-path regex also misses the subsequent bare target. Bash enters tests/golden and writes there without authorization. The new cases cover individual -P and -L options, not valid clusters.
  Recommendation: Parse clusters of supported cd option characters before consuming -- and the operand. Add BLOCK cases into guarded directories and PASS cases into unrelated directories using clustered options.
- [high] Removing non-in-place sed targets introduces an explicit write bypass (.claude/hooks/guard-oracle.sh:205-211)
  `cd tests/golden && sed -n 'w probe.hpp' /dev/null` now passes: there is no in-place flag or shell redirect, so command_arg_targets returns no targets. Sed's w command opens its output file and can truncate it even with empty input. Before e115c75, the generic sed argument handling blocked this command inside the guarded directory. The assumption that sed without -i only reads is false, introducing a regression at the human-owned oracle boundary.
  Recommendation: Recognize explicit sed script output targets, including w and substitution w flags, or conservatively handle write-capable scripts while allowing demonstrated read-only forms. Add a non-in-place protected-output BLOCK case and an unrelated-output PASS case.
- [medium] Sed expression text is mistaken for an in-place option (.claude/hooks/guard-oracle.sh:149-157)
  R-008 remains incomplete: `cd tests/golden && sed -eiprobe nesc/case1_dragless_sphere.hpp` only inserts text into stdout, but sed_is_inplace scans the attached -e expression for the letter i and classifies it as in-place editing. The helper then blocks the protected input path. It likewise lacks option-argument and -- boundaries. Existing read cases use separated expressions and cannot expose this false block.
  Recommendation: Parse sed options with argument consumption: stop scanning a cluster when -e or -f consumes its remainder, consume separate option arguments, and honor --. Add read-only attached-expression cases alongside genuine clustered -i cases.

Next steps:
- Have the builder address these guard defects and add both-direction regression cases.
- Run the authorized validation and request explicit re-verification of R-006, R-007, R-008 and the new findings.
